1. What are Terraform variables?

Terraform variables are input values that allow us to make our Terraform configuration reusable.
Without a variable:

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  instance_type = "t3.large"
}

The value t3.large is fixed.

With a variable:

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  instance_type = var.instance_type
}

Now the value can be provided from outside.
Variable
   ↓
var.instance_type
   ↓
EC2 instance_type

2. Why do we use variables?
We use variables so we don't hard-code values in our Terraform code.
For example, suppose you have:

Development → t3.small
Testing     → t3.medium
Production  → t3.large

Instead of creating three different main.tf files, you can use one:

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  instance_type = var.instance_type
}

Then provide different values:

Development → t3.small
Production  → t3.large



4. What is terraform.tfvars?
terraform.tfvars is commonly used to provide values for input variables.
Suppose you declared:
variable "instance_type" {
  type = string
}

Then create:  terraform.tfvars
with:  instance_type = "t3.large"
Then your resource:
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  instance_type = var.instance_type
}

5. Difference between var.variable_name and local.variable_name
var
var is used to access an input variable.
Example:
variable "instance_type" {
  default = "t3.large"
}
Use:  var.instance_type
Meaning:  Get the value of the input variable instance_type.

local
local is used to access a local value defined inside Terraform.
Example:
locals {
  environment = "production"
  name        = "web-server"
}
Use:
local.environment
local.name

6. What are variable types?
Terraform supports several common types.
1. string
Text:
variable "instance_type" {
  type = string
}
Value:  instance_type = "t3.large"

2. number
Numbers:
variable "instance_count" {
  type = number
}

Value:  instance_count = 3

3. bool
True/false:

variable "enable_monitoring" {
  type = bool
}
Value: enable_monitoring = true

4. list
Multiple ordered values:
variable "availability_zones" {
  type = list(string)
}

Value:
availability_zones = [
  "ap-south-1a",
  "ap-south-1b"
]

5. map
Key-value pairs:
variable "tags" {
  type = map(string)
}

Value:
tags = {
  Environment = "prod"
  Team        = "devops"
}

6. object
Structured data with specific attributes:
variable "server" {
  type = object({
    name = string
    size = string
  })
}

Example:
server = {
  name = "web"
  size = "t3.large"
}
Common types to remember
string
number
bool
list
map
set
object
tuple

7. What is a default value?
A default value is the value Terraform uses if no other value is provided for the variable.
Example:
variable "instance_type" {
  type    = string
  default = "t3.micro"
}
If you don't provide anything:
instance_type
       ↓
t3.micro

Terraform uses: t3.micro



8. What is a sensitive variable?
A sensitive variable contains information that should not normally be displayed in Terraform CLI output.
For example:
variable "db_password" {
  type      = string
  sensitive = true
}

Then:  db_password = "MySecretPassword"
Terraform will try to redact the value in CLI output.
You may see something like:   (sensitive value)
instead of the actual password.

⚠️ Important
sensitive = true does not encrypt the value in the Terraform state.
State can still contain sensitive values.
Therefore:   Protect your Terraform state carefully, especially when using remote state.


9. How do you pass variables from the command line?
There are several ways. The most direct is -var.
Suppose:
variable "instance_type" {
  type = string
}

You can run:  terraform plan -var="instance_type=t3.large"
Or:
terraform apply -var="instance_type=t3.large"
Terraform receives:
instance_type
      ↓
t3.large

Then:  instance_type = var.instance_type
uses:  t3.large
You can pass multiple variables
terraform plan \
  -var="instance_type=t3.large" \
  -var="environment=production"



⭐ One complete example
variables.tf
variable "instance_type" {
  type    = string
  default = "t3.micro"
}

variable "environment" {
  type = string
}
main.tf
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = "ami-123456"
  instance_type = var.instance_type

  tags = {
    Environment = var.environment
  }
}
terraform.tfvars
instance_type = "t3.large"
environment   = "production"

Then:

terraform plan

Terraform gets:

variables.tf
     ↓
Defines variables

terraform.tfvars
     ↓
Provides values

var.instance_type
     ↓
t3.large

var.environment
     ↓
production

     ↓

AWS EC2


So why do we need both?
Because they answer different questions:
type
 ↓
"What type of value can I give?"

default
 ↓
"What value should Terraform use if I give nothing?"





1. What is local?
local is used to access a local value that you define inside Terraform using a locals block.

Example:
locals {
  environment = "production"
  project     = "myapp"
}

Now you can use these values as:

local.environment
local.project



locals.tf
locals {
  environment = "production"
  project     = "myapp"
}
main.tf
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = "ami-123456"
  instance_type = "t3.large"

  tags = {
    Name        = "${local.project}-web"
    Environment = local.environment
  }
}



7. Example using both var and local
variables.tf
variable "environment" {
  type = string
}

variable "instance_type" {
  type = string
}
terraform.tfvars
environment  = "production"
instance_type = "t3.large"
locals.tf
locals {
  server_name = "${var.environment}-web"
}
main.tf
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = "ami-123456"
  instance_type = var.instance_type

  tags = {
    Name        = local.server_name
    Environment = var.environment
  }
}



8. Very important: local can use var

This is where locals become powerful.

You can do:

locals {
  server_name = "${var.environment}-web"
}

Here:

var.environment
       ↓
"production"
       ↓
local.server_name
       ↓
"production-web"

So a local value can be calculated from variables.

You can also use expressions:

locals {
  common_tags = {
    Environment = var.environment
    Project     = var.project
    ManagedBy   = "Terraform"
  }
}

Then many resources can use:

tags = local.common_tags




9. Why is this useful?

Imagine you have 3 resources:

EC2
S3
RDS

All need the same tags.

Without locals:

tags = {
  Environment = var.environment
  Project     = var.project
  ManagedBy   = "Terraform"
}

You repeat this three times.

With locals:

locals {
  common_tags = {
    Environment = var.environment
    Project     = var.project
    ManagedBy   = "Terraform"
  }
}

Then:

resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  tags = local.common_tags
}
resource "aws_s3_bucket" "data" {
  tags = local.common_tags
}
resource "aws_db_instance" "database" {
  tags = local.common_tags
}

Much cleaner.
