1. What are Terraform locals?
Locals are variables defined inside Terraform configuration to store values that we want to reuse.
Example:

locals {
  environment = "dev"
  project     = "myapp"
}

Use them like:
resource "aws_instance" "web" {
  ami           = "ami-123456"
  instance_type = "t3.micro"

  tags = {
    Name        = local.project
    Environment = local.environment
  }
}

Here:
local.project
local.environment

are references to the values defined in the locals block.

2. Why use locals?
Mainly to avoid repeating the same value and make the Terraform code easier to maintain.

Without locals:

tags = {
  Project     = "myapp"
  Environment = "dev"
}

# somewhere else
name = "myapp-dev"

# somewhere else
bucket = "myapp-dev"

With locals:

locals {
  project     = "myapp"
  environment = "dev"
  name        = "${local.project}-${local.environment}"
}

Then:

tags = {
  Project     = local.project
  Environment = local.environment
}

bucket = local.name

So if you change "myapp" once, the places using local.project automatically use the new value.

3. Difference between variables and locals
Variables	Locals
Used to receive input	Used to define/reuse values internally
Can be supplied from outside	Defined inside Terraform configuration
User/CI/CD can provide different values	Usually calculated or organized from existing values
Referenced as var.name	Referenced as local.name
Example

Variable:
variable "environment" {
  default = "dev"
}

You can provide a different value:

terraform apply -var="environment=prod"

So the variable is input.

Local:

locals {
  environment = var.environment
  name        = "myapp-${local.environment}"
}

Here local.name is calculated internally from the variable.

Easy interview answer

Variables are used to accept input into Terraform, while locals are used to store and reuse values or expressions internally within the Terraform configuration.
